+  "description": "The one agreement between agents and the memory tool: the commands to find memory and to update it, what each takes and gives back, and how each fails. A request is a JSON object; on the command line its fields are the flags, and on a server they are the request body. A response is always a JSON object with ok, contract_version and command. Plain-words guide: ../README.md. Version 2 (#657): a lookup of Garura or Phoenix memory works without an issue; a glob never steps outside its tier; Phoenix memory is read only at its approved version; a file read from Phoenix memory is saved into Garura memory. Version 3 (#658): every memory update lands in an issue's short-term memory — moving memory up is the /learn-memory and /promote-memory plays, not this tool; an update says whether it blocks the work; a person decides each update in the review tool; a used file is never saved over a different Garura file.",
+  "tiers": {
+    "short-term": "One issue's own memory: the plans, specs and context of the work on that issue. Root: the folder that holds one folder per issue. A lookup that names an issue reads short-term memory only in that issue's folder ('<issue>/'), never in another issue's; otherwise another issue's notes would answer first, and Garura and Phoenix memory would never be reached. A lookup with no issue has no short-term memory. Every memory update lands here.",
+    "garura": "Garura memory: the project's long-term memory. Holds the subset of Phoenix memory the project pulled, and what the project makes itself (product model, intents, decisions, answers).",
+    "phoenix": "Phoenix memory: the global memory and the superset. Its own git repository until Griffin serves it. Read only at its approved version: the commit the setting phoenix_ref names. A change reaches it when a person approves it outside Garura; until then (on a branch, or edited on disk) the tool does not read it."
